\date{2026 Edition}

\begin{document}
\maketitle
\tableofcontents

\input{chapters/overview}
\input{chapters/design}
\input{chapters/evaluation}

\appendix

\chapter{Inspection Log Template}
A blank log sheet carries the fields crews actually fill in: date, crew,
span type, and a condition code per component.
The template is copied one page per bridge per visit.

\chapter{Torque Values}
Bolt checks use the torque table reproduced here from the hardware
supplier datasheet.
Values assume dry threads unless the table row says otherwise.

\end{document}

\chapter{Program Overview}
The cooperative maintains forty-one trail bridges across three ranger
districts, and every span gets a condition code twice a year.
\section{Scope of Inspections}
A routine visit covers the deck, the stringers, the abutments, and the
hardware that ties them together.
\subsection{Routine Visits}
Routine visits are visual and tactile: sound the timber, torque the
bolts, photograph anything that changed.
\subsection{Triggered Visits}
A flood, a windthrow, or a hiker report schedules a triggered visit
within ten days.
\section{Condition Codes}
Codes run from one to four, where one is sound and four closes the
bridge until a crew signs off.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\fbox{\rule{0pt}{45pt}\rule{110pt}{0pt}}
\caption{Condition codes assigned per component on the standard form.}
\label{fig:codes}
\end{figure}

Recording the code per component, rather than per bridge, is what lets
the office spot a stringer that drifts across visits.

\chapter{Field Kit}
The kit fits in two crates and a torpedo level, and nothing in it needs
a power outlet.
\section{Hand Tools}
A socket set, a scraper, a sounding hammer, and a moisture meter cover
nearly every routine check.
\section{Consumables}
Flagging tape, log sheets, and pencil refills live in the lid crate so a
crew can restock without unpacking.

\begin{table}[b]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
Item & Quantity \\
\hline
Socket set & 1 \\
Sounding hammer & 2 \\
Moisture meter & 1 \\
Log sheets & 40 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Standard crate contents for a two-person crew.}
\label{tab:kit}
\end{table}

Crews sign the log sheet against this table before leaving the
trailhead, which keeps missing tools a trailhead problem.

\chapter{Season Review}
At the end of the season the office compares condition codes across
visits and across districts.
\section{Trend Flags}
A component that drops a code between spring and autumn visits gets a
flag and a closer look the next spring.
\section{Reporting}
The district summary goes to the ranger office with the flagged
components listed first.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\fbox{\rule{0pt}{45pt}\rule{110pt}{0pt}}
\caption{Flagged components by district at season close.}
\label{fig:flags}
\end{figure}

The summary is deliberately short: one page of flags, one page of
photographs, and the log sheets behind them.

